1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,158
A man cuts a rock to find crystals, and ruins a diamond saw blade.

2
00:00:05,158 --> 00:00:11,225
Inside the stone is a white ceramic cylinder with a thin metal rod down its middle.

3
00:00:11,225 --> 00:00:13,199
It looks like a spark plug.

4
00:00:13,199 --> 00:00:17,366
The rock, he is told, is five hundred thousand years old.

5
00:00:17,833 --> 00:00:27,160
For sixty years, the Coso artifact has been sold as proof of a lost civilization, ancient astronauts, even time travelers.

6
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:30,141
One lab-style test finally answered it.

7
00:00:30,141 --> 00:00:31,899
It took a single X-ray.

8
00:00:32,366 --> 00:00:35,664
Tonight we read the scan like a radiologist.

9
00:00:35,664 --> 00:00:38,961
History, findings, differential, impression.

10
00:00:38,961 --> 00:00:42,184
Facts first, then theories, then a verdict.

11
00:00:42,184 --> 00:00:44,133
This is the Coso artifact.

12
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:45,871
The Coso artifact.

13
00:00:45,871 --> 00:00:47,568
Evidence against theory.

14
00:00:47,568 --> 00:00:49,900
Dim the lights and read the film.

15
00:00:54,366 --> 00:00:54,942
History.

16
00:00:54,942 --> 00:00:57,824
February thirteenth, nineteen sixty-one.

17
00:00:57,824 --> 00:01:11,366
Three rockhounds, Wallace Lane, Virginia Maxey and Mike Mikesell, are hunting for geodes on a peak near Olancha, California, about three hundred forty feet above the dry bed of Owens Lake.

18
00:01:11,833 --> 00:01:18,058
They run a little rock shop, the LM and V Rockhounds Gem and Gift Shop, and they need stock.

19
00:01:18,058 --> 00:01:22,997
They fill their sacks all morning, then tip everything together at lunch.

20
00:01:22,997 --> 00:01:27,666
That detail matters later: nobody can say which rock came from where.

21
00:01:28,133 --> 00:01:35,965
The next day, in the shop workroom, Mikesell picks up one lumpy nodule and slices it with a nearly new diamond saw.

22
00:01:35,965 --> 00:01:39,166
It looks like any geode he has cut open before.

23
00:01:39,633 --> 00:01:42,027
There is no cavity, and no crystals.

24
00:01:42,027 --> 00:01:52,471
Instead the blade has cut across a perfectly round section of hard white material, like porcelain, with a two-millimeter shaft of bright metal at its center.

25
00:01:52,471 --> 00:01:54,400
The shaft sticks to a magnet.

26
00:01:54,866 --> 00:01:55,825
More oddities.

27
00:01:55,825 --> 00:02:00,688
Around the ceramic is a hexagonal layer, and a ring of decaying copper.

28
00:02:00,688 --> 00:02:07,400
The outer crust holds bits of shell, and two small metal lumps that look like a nail and a washer.

29
00:02:07,866 --> 00:02:10,585
Maxey wrote about it to Desert Magazine.

30
00:02:10,585 --> 00:02:19,966
In her letter, she said a geologist had examined the shells and judged that the stone needed at least five hundred thousand years to form.

31
00:02:20,433 --> 00:02:22,421
Notice what she did not say.

32
00:02:22,421 --> 00:02:24,266
No name for the geologist.

33
00:02:24,266 --> 00:02:24,976
No report.

34
00:02:24,976 --> 00:02:25,686
No method.

35
00:02:25,686 --> 00:02:30,585
And in nineteen sixty-one, there was no way to date a rock like this.

36
00:02:30,585 --> 00:02:31,862
Hold that thought.

37
00:02:31,862 --> 00:02:33,566
We will come back to it.

38
00:02:34,033 --> 00:02:35,732
The claim traveled fast.

39
00:02:35,732 --> 00:02:38,776
A rock, a spark plug, half a million years.

40
00:02:38,776 --> 00:02:40,900
It was too good not to repeat.

41
00:02:41,366 --> 00:02:44,400
So here is what we have before any scan.

42
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:46,447
A stone, a cylinder, a rod.

43
00:02:46,447 --> 00:02:50,466
And one very big number: five hundred thousand years.

44
00:02:50,933 --> 00:02:52,595
Start with the number.

45
00:02:52,595 --> 00:02:57,278
A real geologist cannot date a lump of stone by looking at it.

46
00:02:57,278 --> 00:02:59,923
Radiocarbon needs organic material.

47
00:02:59,923 --> 00:03:02,718
Other methods need volcanic minerals.

48
00:03:02,718 --> 00:03:04,833
This nodule offered neither.

49
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:07,525
The geologist was never named.

50
00:03:07,525 --> 00:03:09,974
No paper appeared in any journal.

51
00:03:09,974 --> 00:03:12,867
Today, the age is not a finding at all.

52
00:03:12,867 --> 00:03:16,800
It is a rumor, repeated until it sounded like a fact.

53
00:03:17,266 --> 00:03:22,199
The one detail that gave the age any weight was the shells in the crust.

54
00:03:22,199 --> 00:03:27,133
Shells suggest an ancient sea floor, and that is why the story took off.

55
00:03:27,599 --> 00:03:30,442
And the word geode did a lot of work.

56
00:03:30,442 --> 00:03:36,741
Real geodes take a very long time to grow, so a plug inside one sounds impossible.

57
00:03:36,741 --> 00:03:39,199
But is the stone a geode at all?

58
00:03:39,666 --> 00:03:44,902
A real geode has a thin hard shell of silica and a lining of crystals.

59
00:03:44,902 --> 00:03:46,623
This stone had neither.

60
00:03:46,623 --> 00:03:50,139
Maxey herself called the coating hardened clay.

61
00:03:50,139 --> 00:03:53,580
Its hardness is about three on the Mohs scale.

62
00:03:53,580 --> 00:03:55,599
Silica shell is far harder.

63
00:03:56,066 --> 00:03:59,845
And remember the nail and the washer stuck in the crust.

64
00:03:59,845 --> 00:04:07,266
If a modern nail is glued into the same coating as the shells, the shells cannot tell you the plug is ancient.

65
00:04:07,266 --> 00:04:10,033
Nothing in that crust was sealed in time.

66
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:12,937
Which brings up a simpler question.

67
00:04:12,937 --> 00:04:15,445
Who was in these hills with engines?

68
00:04:15,445 --> 00:04:26,033
Mining operations worked this very mountain range early in the twentieth century, and mines in those years ran on brand new internal combustion engines.

69
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:33,166
The first person to X-ray the nodule was Ron Calais, a creationist who collected strange finds.

70
00:04:33,166 --> 00:04:38,500
His film showed the white cylinder and a pale, spring-like shape at the top.

71
00:04:38,966 --> 00:04:41,654
That spring was a gift to the legend.

72
00:04:41,654 --> 00:04:49,500
It did not look like a plug of the nineteen sixties, so writers called it a coil, a helix, even a capacitor.

73
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:51,680
One suggested superconductors.

74
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:53,133
Another, an antenna.

75
00:04:53,600 --> 00:05:01,160
In nineteen sixty-nine, the magazine of the Fort Society, INFO Journal, suggested it was a corroded spark plug.

76
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:06,882
The editor's brother said he was thunderstruck, because all the parts seemed to fit.

77
00:05:06,882 --> 00:05:09,266
Then they got stuck on that spring.

78
00:05:09,733 --> 00:05:12,998
Meanwhile Wallace Lane kept the stone at home.

79
00:05:12,998 --> 00:05:20,451
He refused to let anyone study it, and kept a standing offer to sell it for twenty-five thousand dollars.

80
00:05:20,451 --> 00:05:23,433
The scan was the only evidence anyone had.

81
00:05:23,899 --> 00:05:25,366
It reached television.

82
00:05:25,366 --> 00:05:33,166
A nineteen seventies documentary series hosted by Leonard Nimoy showed it for a few seconds at the end of an episode.

83
00:05:33,166 --> 00:05:36,899
Books followed, and every retelling added a little more.

84
00:05:37,366 --> 00:05:41,919
One book, Secrets of the Lost Races, helped make the stone a classic.

85
00:05:41,919 --> 00:05:45,417
A researcher later claimed he ghost-wrote most of it.

86
00:05:45,417 --> 00:05:50,299
Later authors cited that book, and each other, but never the stone itself.

87
00:05:50,766 --> 00:05:53,676
Which brings us to nineteen ninety-nine.

88
00:05:53,676 --> 00:06:00,732
Two geologists, Pierre Stromberg and Paul Heinrich, wanted to know what that X-ray really showed.

89
00:06:00,732 --> 00:06:02,914
They did what nobody had done.

90
00:06:02,914 --> 00:06:05,533
They asked an expert on spark plugs.

91
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:07,083
Not an engineer.

92
00:06:07,083 --> 00:06:07,896
A collector.

93
00:06:07,896 --> 00:06:16,433
The Spark Plug Collectors of America, a club of people who can name a plug from its shape the way birdwatchers name a sparrow.

94
00:06:16,899 --> 00:06:23,298
On September ninth, nineteen ninety-nine, the club's president, Chad Windham, called Stromberg.

95
00:06:23,298 --> 00:06:25,857
He thought the letter must be a prank.

96
00:06:25,857 --> 00:06:30,033
In his words, he knew what it was the moment he saw the X-ray.

97
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:34,237
His reading: a Champion spark plug, nineteen twenties.

98
00:06:34,237 --> 00:06:44,066
Ten days later, the club's founder, Bill Bond, who keeps more than two thousand plugs in his private museum, called and said exactly the same.

99
00:06:44,533 --> 00:06:48,498
Windham mailed two real plugs of that era for comparison.

100
00:06:48,498 --> 00:06:53,159
He had chipped the brass cap off one, to show what lies underneath.

101
00:06:53,159 --> 00:06:57,333
Compare that to the film, and every strange part has a name.

102
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:00,883
The white cylinder is the ceramic insulator.

103
00:07:00,883 --> 00:07:08,101
The metal rod down the center is the electrode, made of iron alloy, which is why it sticks to a magnet.

104
00:07:08,101 --> 00:07:12,866
The hexagon is the steel body that holds it, shaped to fit a wrench.

105
00:07:13,333 --> 00:07:18,898
The copper that looked so exotic is a pair of copper rings with a gasket between them.

106
00:07:18,898 --> 00:07:25,433
Porcelain and steel expand at different rates when the engine heats, so the plug needed that cushion.

107
00:07:25,899 --> 00:07:27,435
And the famous spring?

108
00:07:27,435 --> 00:07:33,157
Champion plugs of that era often wore a brass top hat with a nut screwed in place.

109
00:07:33,157 --> 00:07:37,763
On a film, that stack of threads and a nut looks like a tiny coil.

110
00:07:37,763 --> 00:07:43,066
The very thing that fooled the believers is a detail of the original design.

111
00:07:43,533 --> 00:07:49,531
Plugs of that design lived in Ford Model T and Model A engines, millions of cars.

112
00:07:49,531 --> 00:07:57,899
Dropped by a prospector, a truck driver, or a mine worker, one lost plug is the most ordinary thing in the world.

113
00:07:58,366 --> 00:08:00,873
The film also shows what time did.

114
00:08:00,873 --> 00:08:04,559
The iron parts have rotted away almost completely.

115
00:08:04,559 --> 00:08:07,213
The brass top hat is nearly perfect.

116
00:08:07,213 --> 00:08:12,300
That is how brass and iron behave when they sit in damp salty ground.

117
00:08:12,766 --> 00:08:14,759
Rust is a swelling mineral.

118
00:08:14,759 --> 00:08:21,623
As iron corrodes, it grows an orange-brown crust that glues sand, clay and pebbles to itself.

119
00:08:21,623 --> 00:08:26,347
Conservators see this all the time on old metal from the ground.

120
00:08:26,347 --> 00:08:28,266
They call it a concretion.

121
00:08:28,733 --> 00:08:31,930
And this was the perfect place to grow one.

122
00:08:31,930 --> 00:08:37,431
The dry bed of Owens Lake throws salty dust onto the hills in every storm.

123
00:08:37,431 --> 00:08:39,141
Salt speeds up rusting.

124
00:08:39,141 --> 00:08:41,966
Years, not millennia, would be enough.

125
00:08:42,433 --> 00:08:52,673
A conservation handbook even shows X-rays of modern objects rotted completely to oxide lumps, leaving empty cavities where the metal used to be.

126
00:08:52,673 --> 00:08:55,233
The Coso stone looks just like them.

127
00:08:55,700 --> 00:08:57,764
So the stone is not a geode.

128
00:08:57,764 --> 00:09:09,266
It is an iron oxide concretion, a rusty crust that built up around a spark plug someone lost on that mountain, a few decades before the rockhounds found it.

129
00:09:09,733 --> 00:09:11,367
Differential diagnosis.

130
00:09:11,367 --> 00:09:16,625
Every good report lists the alternatives and rules them out one at a time.

131
00:09:16,625 --> 00:09:23,233
Let us do the same for the lost civilization, the visitors from space, and the time traveler.

132
00:09:23,700 --> 00:09:26,670
Alternative one: an ancient civilization.

133
00:09:26,670 --> 00:09:32,250
Maxey herself mentioned Atlantis and Mu, imagined worlds with no known ruins.

134
00:09:32,250 --> 00:09:39,133
But if a lost people had built engines, we would find their forges, their ruins, their garbage.

135
00:09:39,133 --> 00:09:40,799
Not one porcelain plug.

136
00:09:41,266 --> 00:09:48,237
What we do find in the ground, wherever people lived, is dozens of broken pots, post holes and ash.

137
00:09:48,237 --> 00:09:54,433
A technology that mass-produces porcelain insulators would have left a mountain of them.

138
00:09:54,899 --> 00:09:57,515
Alternative two: ancient astronauts.

139
00:09:57,515 --> 00:10:03,691
A visitor crossing the stars with an engine that needs spark plugs is a strange idea.

140
00:10:03,691 --> 00:10:06,089
These are the plugs of a Model T.

141
00:10:06,089 --> 00:10:10,666
They burn gasoline in a fixed pattern, nothing like a starship.

142
00:10:11,133 --> 00:10:14,455
Alternative three: a time traveler dropped it.

143
00:10:14,455 --> 00:10:18,933
That is fun, and impossible to disprove, which is the problem.

144
00:10:18,933 --> 00:10:22,833
An idea that nothing can test explains nothing at all.

145
00:10:23,299 --> 00:10:25,080
Alternative four: a hoax.

146
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:28,142
Spark plug collectors say they love pranks.

147
00:10:28,142 --> 00:10:35,121
But the club did not exist until nineteen seventy-five, and none of the three finders ever joined.

148
00:10:35,121 --> 00:10:38,966
Researchers found no sign that anyone planned a trick.

149
00:10:39,433 --> 00:10:41,899
What probably happened is more human.

150
00:10:41,899 --> 00:10:44,633
Three sincere people found something odd.

151
00:10:44,633 --> 00:10:46,766
One person guessed a big number.

152
00:10:46,766 --> 00:10:50,633
The guess became a fact, and nobody went back and checked.

153
00:10:51,100 --> 00:10:55,129
Alternative five: that the X-ray is hiding something.

154
00:10:55,129 --> 00:10:57,334
In 2018 that was settled too.

155
00:10:57,334 --> 00:11:06,533
Stromberg was contacted by the family of one of the finders and finally got to inspect the real stone, not just the film.

156
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:11,389
He brought along a geologist from the University of Washington.

157
00:11:11,389 --> 00:11:15,499
They confirmed it again, a nineteen twenties Champion plug.

158
00:11:15,499 --> 00:11:21,700
And the geologist could find no sign of the fossil shells that had made the stone famous.

159
00:11:22,166 --> 00:11:30,020
Today the stone sits in a glass case at the Pacific Science Center in Seattle, in an exhibit called What Is Reality.

160
00:11:30,020 --> 00:11:35,166
The most honest label it could have is a spark plug that rusted into a rock.

161
00:11:35,633 --> 00:11:38,644
And that fits what the film said all along.

162
00:11:38,644 --> 00:11:40,465
Miners worked those hills.

163
00:11:40,465 --> 00:11:43,407
Cars and trucks bounced along those roads.

164
00:11:43,407 --> 00:11:48,100
One plug fell out, or was thrown away, and the desert did the rest.

165
00:11:48,566 --> 00:11:50,452
The legend still circulates.

166
00:11:50,452 --> 00:12:01,433
Writers repeat the age without the source, and several creationist authors kept presenting it even after the identification was sent to them, without a correction.

167
00:12:01,899 --> 00:12:05,062
This is how an out-of-place artifact is made.

168
00:12:05,062 --> 00:12:10,543
A real object, a missing detail, a confident guess, and a chain of retellings.

169
00:12:10,543 --> 00:12:15,533
Each link cites the one before, and none of them goes back to the rock.

170
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,172
Why does it work?

171
00:12:17,172 --> 00:12:20,277
Because the first impression is irresistible.

172
00:12:20,277 --> 00:12:23,933
A perfect ceramic cylinder in a stone looks designed.

173
00:12:23,933 --> 00:12:29,866
Our brains are trained to see design, and a rusty crust hides the story that ended it.

174
00:12:30,333 --> 00:12:35,910
There is a famous cousin, the London Hammer, an iron hammer in a lump of rock.

175
00:12:35,910 --> 00:12:43,633
Same pattern: ordinary human tool, plus mineral crust, plus a missing date, plus a long chain of retellings.

176
00:12:44,100 --> 00:12:46,814
Real mysteries do exist in archaeology.

177
00:12:46,814 --> 00:12:49,250
But they hold up under measurement.

178
00:12:49,250 --> 00:12:56,699
When an anomaly dissolves with a single X-ray and a phone call, the interesting question is not what it is.

179
00:12:56,699 --> 00:13:00,666
It is why we wanted so badly for it to be something else.

180
00:13:01,133 --> 00:13:05,533
And to be fair to the finders, the legend was not their invention.

181
00:13:05,533 --> 00:13:08,066
They found something odd, and said so.

182
00:13:08,066 --> 00:13:12,600
The pages after that were written by people who never saw the stone.

183
00:13:13,066 --> 00:13:14,640
There is a quiet irony.

184
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:21,481
Half a million years of history was claimed for a thing that has a factory, a brand name and a year.

185
00:13:21,481 --> 00:13:24,833
The truth is a lot more specific than the legend.

186
00:13:25,299 --> 00:13:26,786
Time to score the case.

187
00:13:26,786 --> 00:13:29,500
Evidence on one side, theory on the other.

188
00:13:29,966 --> 00:13:33,782
A stone cut open in nineteen sixty-one: evidence.

189
00:13:33,782 --> 00:13:37,598
A porcelain and metal object inside it: evidence.

190
00:13:37,598 --> 00:13:43,673
A film that matches a nineteen twenties Champion plug part for part: evidence.

191
00:13:43,673 --> 00:13:47,100
A rusty crust that grows in years: evidence.

192
00:13:47,566 --> 00:13:51,900
A five hundred thousand year age: no source, no method.

193
00:13:51,900 --> 00:13:52,846
A geode: no.

194
00:13:52,846 --> 00:13:54,816
Fossil shells: not found.

195
00:13:54,816 --> 00:14:00,254
A lost civilization, a starship engine, a time traveler: no evidence.

196
00:14:00,254 --> 00:14:02,066
A hoax: no sign of one.

197
00:14:02,533 --> 00:14:03,357
The verdict.

198
00:14:03,357 --> 00:14:07,409
Not aliens, not Atlantis, and not half a million years old.

199
00:14:07,409 --> 00:14:17,366
The Coso artifact is a nineteen twenties Champion spark plug that rusted into a lump, and the only thing that was ancient about it was the story.

200
00:14:17,833 --> 00:14:19,941
And that is the better story.

201
00:14:19,941 --> 00:14:25,392
One X-ray, one phone call to a hobbyist, and sixty years of myth collapsed.

202
00:14:25,392 --> 00:14:29,099
Curiosity beat the legend, the way it usually does.

203
00:14:29,566 --> 00:14:37,713
Next case: in Peru, small mummified bodies with long skulls and three fingers were shown to the world as aliens.

204
00:14:37,713 --> 00:14:39,314
Then labs ran the DNA.

205
00:14:39,314 --> 00:14:42,733
What they found was stranger than a spark plug.

206
00:14:43,200 --> 00:14:45,687
Spark plug, geode, or something else?

207
00:14:45,687 --> 00:14:51,333
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
